  "query_preview": "..The calendar shows mid September but still no \ntransition to drier non monsoonal weather for the upcoming work week \nas deep layer southerly flow continues. Moisture levels remain quite \nelevated today with PWAT values ranging from 1.3 to 1.7 inches \n(lowest near the NM border and highest west of Tucson). These values \nare about 150 to 200 percent of normal and with the southerly flow \nremaining this week, the above normal moisture isn't going anywhere. \nNow with the abundant moisture in place, we start looking for other \natmospheric signals for more active days. The sun angle in September \ncertainly isn't as good now compared to July or August so it's \nreally helpful to have other upper level support for better storm \nchances. Not much upper level support for today, though isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms will continue through the \nafternoon into early evening mainly from Sells eastward. Heavy rain \nwill be the main threat. \n\nMonday is shaping up to be a more active day. While we can't rule \nout a bit of morning convection, HREF/REFS/UofA WRF-HRRR are leaning \nto a more active afternoon into evening as the combination of a \nample instability (MLCAPE 1000-2000+ J/KG) and some modest \nmid/upper level support result in more widespread shower and \nthunderstorm chances. Given the anomalously high moisture and broad \nwetter antecedent conditions over the past few days it won't take as \nmuch rain for to get notable responses in our washes and rivers. The \nHREF has a large 50 to 90 percent area of 1 inch or greater of rain \n(40-km neighborhood probability) from Tucson eastward with about 10 \npercent for 2 inches of more. Given the increasing confidence for \nscattered to numerous thunderstorms Monday and heavy rain potential \nalong with previously mentioned wet antecedent conditions, decided \nto hoist a Flash Flood Watch from late Monday morning through Monday \nevening on a line from Sasabe-Eloy eastward but excluding the White \nMountains where the setup isn't as favorable up there. \n\nBeyond Monday, it's looking like an active week. Tuesday will likely \nbe a bit more down compared to Monday, though still will have \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms. Wednesday into \nThursday are looking like another up period with some enhancement of \na disturbance coming in from the south. Westerly flow aloft tries to \nestablish itself Friday into next weekend which should result in \ndecreasing precipitation chances especially from Tucson westward but \nstill plenty of time to sort out those details. \n\nTemperatures this week will range from near normal to up to a few \ndegrees below normal.",
